[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Technical Tutorial: Event Sourcing & CQRS in Microservices"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"As microservices grow in complexity, traditional CRUD architectures often struggle with scalability, data consistency, and auditability. Event Sourcing and Command Query Responsibility Segregation (CQRS) are architectural patterns designed to address these challenges by decoupling write operations from read operations and storing state as a sequence of immutable events."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of CQRS"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"CQRS separates the data modification model (Command) from the data retrieval model (Query). By splitting these concerns, systems can scale reads and writes independently, optimize database schemas for specific workloads, and reduce contention."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Commands: Represent write operations that mutate state (e.g., CreateOrder, UpdateInventory). They typically return nothing or a success status and can trigger domain events."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Queries: Represent read operations that fetch data without modifying state (e.g., GetOrderDetails). They return DTOs optimized for UI rendering."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Event Sourcing"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Event Sourcing ensures that every state change in an application is captured as an immutable event object stored in an append-only event store. Instead of persisting just the current state of an entity, the system persists the full history of changes."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Event Store: A specialized database optimized for appending and streaming events."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"State Reconstruction: The current state of an entity is derived by replaying its historical events from the event store."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Command Handler"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a conceptual example of a Command Handler in C# handling an order creation command and emitting an event."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public class OrderCommandHandler {
    private readonly IEventStore _eventStore;

    public OrderCommandHandler(IEventStore eventStore) {
        _eventStore = eventStore;
    }

    public async Task Handle(CreateOrderCommand command) {
        var orderId = Guid.NewGuid();
        var orderCreatedEvent = new OrderCreatedEvent(orderId, command.CustomerId, command.Items);

        await _eventStore.SaveEventsAsync(orderId, new[] { orderCreatedEvent });
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Projecting Events to Read Models"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because event stores are optimized for writes, CQRS relies on projections to build read-optimized databases (such as MongoDB or Read-Optimized SQL). Event projectors consume the event stream and update the query models asynchronously."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"public class OrderProjectionHandler {
    private readonly IReadDatabase _readDb;

    public OrderProjectionHandler(IReadDatabase readDb) {
        _readDb = readDb;
    }

    public async Task HandleEvent(OrderCreatedEvent evt) {
        var readModel = new OrderSummaryDto {
            Id = evt.OrderId,
            CustomerId = evt.CustomerId,
            Status = "Created",
            TotalItems = evt.Items.Count
        };
        await _readDb.InsertAsync(readModel);
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Advantages and Challenges"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Advantage: Complete audit trail and historical time-travel debugging capabilities out of the box."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Advantage: Independent scalability and tailored performance tuning for reads and writes."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Challenge: Eventual consistency between the write model and read models requires careful handling."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Challenge: Complexity in schema evolution and event versioning over time."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Combining CQRS and Event Sourcing provides a powerful foundation for complex microservices architecture. While it introduces operational complexity, the benefits in traceability, decoupled scaling, and domain modeling make it an invaluable pattern for enterprise-grade systems."}]
